1. The text argues that God creates all possibilities, and suffering exists because we, as subjective experiencers, choose to perceive it. It claims that our experience of evil is a personal choice, not an objective reality, and that we can always change our perspective or situation. The main thesis is that suffering is a self-selected perception, not an inherent flaw in creation.

2. I partially agree. The idea that our perception shapes our experience is valid, and some suffering is indeed a matter of perspective or choice. However, I disagree with the claim that all suffering is chosen—physical pain, trauma, and systemic injustice often occur against a person's will, and dismissing them as "choices" can be harmful and dismissive of real harm.

3.  
The All creates, a boundless sea,  
Each wave a choice, a destiny.  
You pick the lens, you set the frame,  
And call the tiger by its name.  

The pain you see, the war, the cry—  
A shadow cast by your own eye.  
Yet move your step, or shift your view,  
The world you fear is made by you. [end of text]


